{"results":[{"url":"https://reversevideosearch.co/","final_url":"https://reversevideosearch.co/","title":"Reverse Video Search: Upload a Clip or Paste a URL","description":"Upload a video, paste a public URL, or search by keywords to find matching pages and investigate original video sources with Reverse Video Search.","language":"en","text":"Drop your video here or click to upload\n\nMP4 · MOV · AVI · WEBM · GIF · Max 50MB · 20 min\n\nSign up and get 5 free credits\n\nSign in to search. Quick search: 5 credits. Optional advanced verification: 15 credits total. Gifted and purchased credits both work.\n\nPrivacy ProtectedResults In SecondsAccount Protected\n\n## Try a frame search before paying\n\nExtract and download frames in your browser—no account, upload or credits needed. Full searches require sign-in; new accounts receive 5 credits for one quick search.\n\nExtract frames for freeSearch a video\n\nSEARCH EXAMPLES\n\n## See what a video search can uncover\n\nExplore three search result examples, from animated scenes and movie clips to clothing spotted in a video.\n\n### Animation clip\n\nAn uploaded animation clip returns candidate pages across TikTok, YouTube and other platforms.\n\n### Movie scene\n\nA video URL leads to image candidates, including a YouTube clip of the same movie.\n\n### Style & products\n\nFrames from a short video surface visually similar clothing and product pages.\n\nSearch beyond the caption\n\n## Turn a clip into a trail of evidence.\n\nA clip can be cropped, mirrored or reposted with a new caption. Start with keyframe candidates, then compare available visual, audio and text clues before deciding what a result supports.\n\nUse it when you need to find the earliest known upload, check whether viral footage is being presented out of context, or understand how an original asset moved across platforms. The result is a focused starting point for human verification, not a black-box verdict. You can compare source pages, dates, captions, edits, and accounts before deciding what the match means.\n\nHow it works\n\n## From one clip to a clearer answer\n\nA practical Reverse Video Search workflow keeps the analysis fast while giving you enough detail to validate every important result.\n\n01\n\n### Add your best source\n\nUse Reverse Video Search with a short, clear clip, a public URL, or keywords describing the footage. Distinctive frames and phrases usually perform best.\n\n02\n\n### Extract the signals\n\nReverse Video Search samples keyframes and can inspect audio, transcripts, subtitles, logos, and on-screen text for additional clues.\n\n03\n\n### Compare likely matches\n\nReverse Video Search checks visual fingerprints against indexed pages and public platforms, then ranks matches by similarity, dates, source quality, and confidence.\n\n04\n\n### Verify before you act\n\nAfter Reverse Video Search, open source links, compare timelines and edits, and save the evidence report before contacting a rights team or publishing a finding.\n\nBuilt for real investigations\n\n## Different questions. A reviewable search workflow.\n\nWhether you protect original work or verify a claim, the same search signals help you replace manual platform-by-platform checking with a repeatable process.\n\n### Creators\n\nUse Reverse Video Search to find unauthorized reposts of tutorials, films, livestreams, and social clips. Document copies, request attribution, or prepare a licensing conversation.\n\n### Journalists\n\nJournalists use Reverse Video Search to trace viral footage to an earlier source and test whether its date, location, or caption is misleading.\n\n### Brands and agencies\n\nBrands use Reverse Video Search to monitor campaign assets, influencer deliverables, and logos for modified ads, fake endorsements, or risky copies.\n\n### Researchers and legal teams\n\nResearchers use Reverse Video Search to build a searchable provenance record with source URLs, dates, thumbnails, edits, and notes.\n\nUse evidence responsibly\n\n## Fast matching, careful conclusions.\n\nReverse Video Search is designed to reduce repetitive searching without overstating what an automated match proves. Treat every result as a lead, preserve the original file, and keep your review process transparent.\n\n### Privacy comes first\n\nBefore Reverse Video Search, review encryption, retention, access, model-training, and deletion policies for private or restricted footage.\n\n### More than one signal\n\nReverse Video Search combines frames, audio, transcript, OCR, watermarks, and timeline clues to reduce false positives and explain a match.\n\n### A match is a starting point\n\nReverse Video Search cannot see every private account, deleted post, regional restriction, or unindexed page. Confirm origin, context, and ownership independently.\n\n## Signals that reveal the original\n\nReverse Video Search combines multiple clues so you can investigate a match instead of guessing from a title or caption.\n\n### Visual fingerprinting\n\nReverse Video Search compares keyframes and near-duplicate scenes that remain recognizable after resizing, compression, cropping, or mirroring.\n\n### Audio fingerprints\n\nReverse Video Search matches a soundtrack, spoken recording, or interview even when the same audio appears in a different edit.\n\n### Text and watermark detection\n\nReverse Video Search reads captions, logos, usernames, and subtitles with OCR to connect a repost to the account behind it.\n\n### Evidence-ready reports\n\nReverse Video Search keeps source URLs, dates, thumbnails, similarity details, and notes together for review or a rights request.\n\n### Privacy-first searches\n\nReverse Video Search makes file limits, retention, and deletion controls clear before you upload sensitive footage for analysis.\n\n### Search email updates\n\nReceive an email when your search finishes, finds no matches, or cannot be completed. Open the results from your inbox.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions About Reverse Video Search\n\nClear answers about Reverse Video Search, matching quality, privacy, and content protection.\n\nWhat is Reverse Video Search?\n\nReverse Video Search compares the content of a video—frames, audio, spoken words, and on-screen text—to find related copies or earlier sources. It is useful when titles, captions, or languages have changed.\n\nCan I search with a video, link, keyword, or screenshot?\n\nYes. Upload a video or a PNG, JPG, or WEBP image, paste a public video URL, or enter descriptive keywords. Image matches are candidate pages, not proof of the original uploader.\n\nHow accurate are the results?\n\nResults depend on index coverage, video quality, and how much a clip was edited. Visual, audio, transcript, and OCR signals improve confidence, but every match should be checked against the source page, date, and context.\n\nIs my uploaded video private?\n\nReview the provider's encryption, retention, model-training, access, and deletion policies before uploading sensitive material. Avoid submitting private or restricted footage without the right permission.\n\nCan Reverse Video Search help with copyright?\n\nIt can help document reposts, source URLs, upload dates, edits, and watermarks before you contact a platform or rights holder. A match is evidence for review, not automatic proof of ownership or infringement.\n\nIs a reverse video search the same as a reverse video lookup?\n\nYes. A reverse video lookup, a video reverse search, and a reverse video search describe the same task: start from a clip instead of a title, then look for earlier or matching copies. The wording changes from person to person; the method — keyframes, audio, spoken phrases, and on-screen text — does not.\n\n## Find the source behind your next video\n\nStart a Reverse Video Search to trace a repost, verify a claim, or protect a valuable piece of content. Upload a clip or paste a public URL and follow the evidence.\n\nStart a video search","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":1007.5026220001746,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}